Title: WATTLE(1)
Authors: Michael Camilleri <mike@inqk.net>
Date: 2026-09-29
Project: Wattle
Version: 0.1.1
---

NAME
====

**wattle** - run Wattle programs and a Wattle REPL

SYNOPSIS
========

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build img** [**-l** _module_ ...] _source_ [_output_]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build exe** [**-r** _mode_] [**-t** _triple_] [_name_]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build lib** [**-r** _mode_] [**-t** _triple_] [_name_]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build web** [**-r** _mode_] [_name_]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **check** [_options_] [_script_]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **gum** [**-d** _directory_] [**-f**] [_module_ ...]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **pkg** _verb_ [_argument_]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] [**run**] [_options_] [_script_ [_args_ ...]]
**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **test** [_options_]
**wattle** **help** [_subcommand_ ...]
**wattle** [**-h** | **-v**]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**wattle** runs programs written in Wattle, a Lisp-like programming language.
Wattle reimplements the virtual machine, compiler and core library from the
Janet programming language in Zig with a syntax inspired by Clojure.

**wattle** has six subcommands. **build** makes an image from source, or
builds the executables and native modules that a project declares. **check**
compiles a script without running it. **gum** copies Gum modules into a
project. **pkg** manages installed packages.
**run** runs a script, evaluates code or starts a REPL (read-eval-print loop).
**test** runs the tests of a project. **help** describes a subcommand.

Each subcommand can be written as its first letter: **b**, **c**, **g**, **p**,
**r** and **t**, and **h** for **help**. ``wattle t`` is the same as ``wattle test``.

**run** is the subcommand when no other is named. Where **wattle** would
otherwise report an unrecognised subcommand or option, or no subcommand, it
reads the rest of the command line as the arguments of **run**. The command
lines ``wattle``, ``wattle hello.wattle`` and ``wattle -e '(+ 1 2)'`` are
therefore the same as ``wattle run``, ``wattle run hello.wattle`` and
``wattle run -e '(+ 1 2)'``. A mistyped subcommand is read as the name of a
script, and a script named after a subcommand or its first letter is run
with ``wattle run`` in front of it.

**-c**, **-C**, **-s**, **-v** and **-h** are read only before the subcommand name. A
subcommand does not read them again.

An option that takes a value can be written ``--eval=code``, and short options
can be combined, as in ``-qr``. ``--`` stops the processing of options.

An unrecognised option, an option missing its value or a missing parameter
prints a message and a pointer to **--help** on standard error and exits with
status 1.

**wattle** exits when the script and any tasks it scheduled on the event loop
have finished.

Options
-------

- **-c**, **--color**:
  Print values, errors and documentation with ANSI colour, and highlight the
  source typed at the REPL. If **-C** is also given, **-C** applies.

- **-C**, **--no-color**:
  Print values, errors and documentation without ANSI colour, and do not
  highlight the source typed at the REPL.

- **-p**, **--prefix** _path_:
  Set ``*prefix*``, the root directory that ``lib/wattle``, ``bin`` and
  ``share/man`` derive from, to _path_. System modules are loaded from
  ``lib/wattle`` under it. This replaces the value taken from `WATTLE_PREFIX`.

- **-v**, **--version**:
  Print the version of Wattle and exit.

- **-h**, **--help**:
  Print a usage summary and exit. Each subcommand also reads **-h**, and
  prints its own usage.

build
-----

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build img** [**-l** _module_ ...] _source_ [_output_]

Load the module at _source_ and write an image of its environment to
_output_. If _output_ is omitted, it is _source_ with ``.wattle`` replaced by
``.wimage``. **run -i** loads the image.

- **-l**, **--lib** _module_:
  Import _module_ into the environment before _source_ is loaded, as **run**
  does. Several **-l** options run in the order given.

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build exe** [**-r** _mode_] [**-t** _triple_] [_name_]

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build lib** [**-r** _mode_] [**-t** _triple_] [_name_]

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **build web** [**-r** _mode_] [_name_]

Build the artifacts declared in the ``info.edn`` in the current directory, or
in its ``pkg`` subdirectory. **build exe** makes a single-file executable for
each ``:exe``, containing the Wattle runtime, the compiled program and the
native modules it links. **build lib** makes a shared library for each
``:lib``. **build web** makes, for each ``:web``, an image of the program, a
Wattle runtime built for WebAssembly that loads an image, and the JavaScript
that runs them in a browser. If _name_ is given, only the artifact of that name
is built. The
results are in ``zig-out`` in the current directory. The project needs no
``build.zig``.

Both commands need Zig on the ``PATH`` and the Wattle package, which
**zig build** installs in ``share/wattle``, under ``*prefix*``. They write a
``build.zig`` and a ``build.zig.zon`` in ``.zig-cache/wattle-build`` and run
**zig build** on them. Each error in ``info.edn``, and a missing Zig, is
reported on one line and the exit status is 1.

The ``info.edn`` declares the artifacts in a vector, in the ``:artifacts`` key:

````
{:name "greeter"
 :artifacts [{:type :lib
              :name "greet"
              :root "greet.zig"}
             {:type :exe
              :name "hello"
              :entry "main.wattle"
              :libs ["greet"]}]}
````

Every artifact has a ``:type``, ``:exe``, ``:lib`` or ``:web``, and a ``:name`` of
letters, digits, ``_`` and ``-`` that is unique in the project. An ``:exe`` has
an ``:entry``, the Wattle file that defines ``main``, and may have ``:libs``,
the names of the ``:lib`` artifacts linked into it. A ``:lib`` has a ``:root``,
the Zig root file of a native module. A ``:web`` has an ``:entry`` and no
other key. Relative paths are relative to the current directory.

**build web** writes ``zig-out/web/<name>/`` with four files: ``wattle-<hash>.wasm``,
the runtime; ``<name>-<hash>.wimage``, the program; ``wasi-<hash>.js``, the
WASI support; and ``<name>.js``, an ES module that exports ``run`` and imports
the support. ``<hash>`` is the same in the three names. It is sixteen
hexadecimal digits of a SHA-256 over the contents of the runtime, the image and
the support, so a change to any of them gives all three new names and files
with the same hash belong together. ``<name>.js`` keeps its name and refers to
the other three. ``load`` fetches
the runtime and the image once, compiles the runtime and returns an object with
``run``. ``run`` takes ``args``, an array of strings, and ``stdin``, a string or
a ``Uint8Array``. It loads the image, calls its ``main`` with the arguments as
they are, so the first is the program's name by the convention of **wattle -i**,
and returns an object with ``status``, ``stdout``, ``stderr`` and ``error``.
Standard input reads ``stdin`` to its end. The first ``run`` starts an instance
of the runtime and later calls use it. A call that traps or exits leaves no
usable instance and the next call starts another, and ``fresh: true`` starts one
for that call. ``run`` exported from the module is ``load`` and one call. Both
take the URLs of the two files as ``wasm`` and ``image`` when they are not
beside the module. The runtime has no parser and no compiler, so
the program cannot call ``eval``, ``parse`` or ``run-context``, and it has no
docstrings or source maps. The runtime is built for ``wasm32-wasi`` and
**build web** takes no **-t**.

- **-r**, **--release** _mode_:
  Optimise for ``safe``, ``fast`` or ``small``. The default is ``safe``, and
  ``small`` for **build web**.

- **-t**, **--target** _triple_:
  Build for _triple_, a Zig target such as ``x86_64-linux-musl``. The default is
  this machine. **build web** does not take it.

check
-----

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **check** [_options_] [_script_]

Compile _script_ without running it and report every error. With no _script_,
check standard input instead. The exit status is 0 after errors are reported,
unless **-b** is given.

- **-e**, **--lint-error** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message stops compilation with an error.
  _level_ is one of ``none``, ``relaxed``, ``normal``, ``strict`` or ``all``,
  written without a leading colon, or a number. These are the numbers 0, 1, 2
  and 3 and infinity. A message is reported when its own level is at most
  _level_, so ``none`` reports nothing and ``all`` reports every message. The
  default is ``none``.

- **-w**, **--lint-warn** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message is printed as a warning. _level_ takes
  the same values as for **-e**. The default is ``normal``.

- **-b**, **--bail**:
  Stop at the first error in the source and exit with status 1.

gum
---

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **gum** [**-d** _directory_] [**-f**] [_module_ ...]

Copy Gum modules into a project. Gum is the set of optional Wattle modules
installed in ``share/wattle/src/gum`` under ``*prefix*``. With no _module_,
**gum** prints the name and a description of each module in two columns, wrapped as **--help** wraps. With one or more,
it copies the files of each, which are its source and its licence, into the
``gum`` directory of _directory_, and prints ``copied`` or ``unchanged`` and
the path of each file. A project imports a copy by its path, as in
``(import ./deps/gum/args :as args)``. A build made without the OS library
does not include **gum**, and it exits with status 1 after saying so.

**gum** checks every _module_ and every destination before it copies a file.
It exits with status 1 having copied nothing if a _module_ is not one of
Gum's, or if a destination file exists with content that differs from its
source. A file with the same content is left as it is.

- **-d**, **--dir** _directory_:
  Copy into ``gum`` under _directory_. The default is ``deps``.

- **-f**, **--force**:
  Replace a destination file that differs from its source.

pkg
---

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **pkg** _verb_ [_argument_]

Manage the packages installed under ``*prefix*``. Modules are installed in
``lib/wattle``, programs in ``bin`` and man pages in ``share/man`` under it. A build made without the
OS library does not include **pkg**, and each verb exits with status 1 after
saying so.

- **install** [_source_ [_ref_]]:
  Install a package. _source_ is a directory, a name ending in ``.tar`` or
  ``.tar.gz``, or a Git repository. A directory is installed as it is. A name
  ending in ``.tar`` or ``.tar.gz`` is downloaded with **curl** and unpacked
  with **tar**. Anything else is cloned with **git**. With no _source_, the
  current directory is installed. If the package contains ``pkg.wattle`` or
  ``pkg/init.wattle``, **pkg** runs the hooks of that script and nothing else.
  Otherwise **pkg** builds each ``:exe`` artifact of ``info.edn`` with
  **build exe**, installs the program in ``bin``, and installs each file in the
  ``:man`` vector of ``info.edn`` in ``share/man``. The section is the digit
  that ends the file name, so ``man/greet.1`` is installed in ``man1``. A
  ``:web`` artifact is not installed, and a package with a ``:lib`` artifact
  needs a script. _ref_ is a branch or tag, and applies only to a Git
  repository.

- **reinstall** _name_:
  Reinstall the installed package _name_. A package installed from a tarball or
  a Git repository is downloaded again.

- **uninstall** _name_:
  Uninstall the package _name_.

- **update**:
  Reinstall every installed package.

- **clean**:
  Uninstall every orphaned package: one marked for automatic removal that no
  other package depends on.

- **list**:
  List the installed packages.

run
---

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] [**run**] [_options_] [_script_ [_args_ ...]]

Options are read until the first argument that does not begin with ``-``.
That argument is the _script_, and every argument after it is passed to the
script without being read as an option. Once the script has run, **wattle**
calls the ``main`` function it defines, if any, with the script's path
followed by _args_. The same list is bound to ``*args*``. With no _script_,
and no **-e**, **run** starts a REPL.

An error that a _script_, a module loaded with **-l** or code given to
**-e** does not handle ends the process with status 1.

The options are applied in a fixed order, whatever order they are written in:
first the settings (**-d**, **-s**, **-r**, **-q** and
**-i**), then **-l**, then **-e**, and last the _script_. A setting therefore
applies to every library, expression and script, and **-l** runs before every
**-e**. Several **-e** options and several **-l** options run in the order
given.

- **-e**, **--eval** _code_:
  Evaluate _code_ as Wattle source. The REPL is not started afterwards unless
  **-r** is also given.

- **-l**, **--lib** _module_:
  Import _module_ into the environment before continuing, without a prefix, so
  its bindings are available to later options, the _script_ and the REPL.

- **-i**, **--img**:
  Treat _script_ as an image made by **build img** rather than as source, load
  it and call its ``main`` function.

- **-r**, **--repl**:
  Start the REPL after the _script_ or the other options have run.

- **-s**, **--stdin**:
  Read REPL input as raw lines from standard input and write prompts to
  standard output. Implies **-r**.

- **-d**, **--debug**:
  Set ``*debug*`` and ``*redef*``. An error in the REPL enters the debugger,
  and a top-level definition can be rebound.

- **-q**, **--quiet**:
  Do not print the banner when the REPL starts.

test
----

**wattle** [**-s** _path_] **test** [_options_]

Run the tests. **test** runs each file ending in ``.wattle`` under ``./test``,
in a process of its own. It prints ``seed:`` and the seed. It then prints one
line for each file, with a mark, the path and the counts of tests and
assertions. It prints the output of a file that failed after its line, each
line after a border that is red when colour is on. It ends with the number of
files and tests and the files that failed. The output of a file that passed is
not printed. The exit status is 1 if a file failed or no file was run. A test
file that uses the Gum test module, ``gum/test.wattle``, reads the dynamic
bindings that **-t**, **-T** and **--seed** set.

- **-f**, **--file** _path_:
  Run only the test file whose path ends with _path_, which is relative to
  ``./test``. It can be repeated. It is not compatible with **-F**.

- **-F**, **--no-file** _path_:
  Do not run the test file whose path ends with _path_. It can be repeated. It
  is not compatible with **-f**.

- **-t**, **--test** _name_:
  Add _name_ to the dynamic binding ``:test/tests`` in each test file. It can
  be repeated. It is not compatible with **-T**.

- **-T**, **--no-test** _name_:
  Add _name_ to the dynamic binding ``:test/skips`` in each test file. It can
  be repeated. It is not compatible with **-t**.

- **--seed** _seed_:
  Use the integer _seed_ to seed the generator that shuffles the test files.
  Each test file also shuffles its tests with it, as **test** sets the dynamic
  binding ``:test/seed``. Without **--seed**, the seed is taken from the clock,
  so each run has a different order. The same seed gives the same order.

- **-b**, **--bail**:
  Stop after the first test file that fails.

- **-e**, **--lint-error** _level_, **-w**, **--lint-warn** _level_:
  Set the lint levels of each test file. They take the same values as for
  **check**.

help
----

**wattle help** [_subcommand_ ...]

Print the usage of the last _subcommand_ named. ``wattle help pkg install``
prints the usage of **pkg install**. With no _subcommand_, **help** reports
an error.

REPL
====

The REPL reads a line at a time and evaluates each form once it is complete.
The prompt shows the line number and any open delimiters. Documentation for a
binding is shown with ``(doc symbol)``, and ``(doc)`` lists the bindings in
scope.

Prompts are written to standard error, or to standard output with **-s**.

ENVIRONMENT
===========

- `WATTLE_PREFIX`:
  The root directory that ``lib/wattle``, ``bin`` and ``share/man`` derive from.
  It becomes ``*prefix*``, and system modules are loaded from ``lib/wattle``
  under it. **-p** replaces it. If unset, ``*prefix*`` is the value the
  build was made with: a path, none, or nothing yet. A build with none loads no
  system modules. A build with nothing yet, which is the default, raises an
  error that names `WATTLE_PREFIX` at the first system module lookup, **gum**
  command or **pkg** command.

- `WATTLE_HISTFILE`:
  A file to keep the REPL's history in. Each form submitted at the REPL on a
  terminal is appended to it, and the forms in it are recalled with the Up
  key in a later session. If unset, history lasts only until the REPL exits.

- `NO_COLOR`:
  If set to a non-empty value, disable colour. Colour is otherwise used when
  both standard output and standard error are terminals.

- `TERM`, `COLORTERM`:
  The terminal has 256 colours if `TERM` contains `256color` or `COLORTERM` is
  set to a non-empty value, and 16 otherwise. With 16, `nil`, `true` and
  `false` are bright magenta and comments are grey rather than orange.

An environment variable set to the empty string is treated as unset.

EXIT STATUS
===========

**wattle** exits `0` on success and `1` if a script, a subcommand or an option
raises an error that is not handled. An error in the REPL is printed and does not end the
session. A program can choose its own status with ``(os/exit status)``.

EXAMPLES
========

Start the REPL:

````
$ wattle
````

Run a script with two arguments:

````
$ wattle hello.wattle Alice Bob
````

Evaluate an expression and print its result:

````
$ wattle -e '(print (+ 1 2 3))'
````

Compile a program into an image and run the image:

````
$ wattle build img hello.wattle hello.wimage
$ wattle run -i hello.wimage Alice
````

Build the executables that ``info.edn`` declares, optimised for size:

````
$ wattle -p /usr/local build exe --release small
````

Check a script for compile errors without running it, stopping at the first:

````
$ wattle check --bail hello.wattle
````

Run the tests in ``./test``, skipping one file:

````
$ wattle test --no-file slow.wattle
````

Install packages from a directory, a tarball and a Git repository, and list the installed packages:

````
$ wattle pkg install ./my-pkg
$ wattle pkg install https://example.com/my-pkg.tar.gz
$ wattle pkg install https://example.com/my-pkg.git v1.2
$ wattle pkg list
````

SEE ALSO
========

Wattle does not yet have a written manual. The core library documents itself:
``(doc symbol)`` prints a binding's documentation in the REPL and ``(doc)``
lists the bindings in scope. Example programs are distributed with the source
at <https://github.com/pyrmont/wattle>.

HISTORY
=======

Wattle began as a fork of Janet, the language created by Calvin Rose. It has
its own version numbering. The binding ``wattle/version`` is the number of a
release, such as 0.1.0, or for a build between releases, DEVEL and the
abbreviated hash of its commit, with -dirty appended when a tracked file
differed from that commit.

